</div><p class="comparison-note">We confirm fit and agree on the engagement before payment. The Audit is paid upfront.</p></div></section>
<section class="section section-soft"><div class="shell recognition-grid"><div><h2 class="section-title">A new tool doesn’t make the decision for you.</h2></div><div class="recognition-copy"><p>Picture a week where the team has worked hard, but a good lead still hasn’t heard back. A customer who used to reorder has gone quiet. The numbers exist somewhere, but pulling them together takes another afternoon.</p><p>It’s easy to jump from that frustration to “we need better software.” Sometimes you do. Sometimes the first fix is much smaller.</p><p>I spent years building software with business owners at Upstack Studio. Now I’m building Second Team. I bring both sides of that work to these conversations: what it takes to build, and what has to be true for the business to benefit.</p><p>We start there. Then we decide what deserves your money and attention.</p></div></div></section>
<section class="section"><div class="shell content-grid"><h2>Is this a fit?</h2><div class="content-body"><h3>You have customers, a team and a problem worth fixing.</h3><p>The best fit is usually a founder-led B2B company with about USD $1M–$20M+ in yearly revenue, 10–200 people and an existing customer base. It is an especially good fit when customers buy more than once and follow-up is inconsistent.</p><p>You can bring the relevant business data and explain how the work happens today. We use that to work out what deserves attention.</p><div class="boundary"><strong>The key point</strong>You have a real business problem. AI is one possible tool, not the starting point.</div></div></div></section>
<section class="section section-soft"><div class="shell"><h2>What happens next</h2><div class="next-steps"><article><h3>We talk about the business.</h3><p>A 30-minute call on Google Meet. You share the problem; we decide whether there’s a fit.</p></article><article><h3>We agree on the right work.</h3><p>If the Audit or advisory makes sense, we agree on scope and payment before you commit.</p></article><article><h3>Then we get into the detail.</h3><p>After payment, you complete the intake. The paid engagement starts with the facts of your business.</p></article></div></div></section>
